  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Same-Frequency Output Property",
    "lesson_core_move": "Recognize that a sinusoidal input to a continuous-time LTI system produces an output sinusoid at the same angular frequency, with frequency response affecting only amplitude and phase.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Same-Frequency Output Property",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Same-Frequency Output Property",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A continuous-time LTI system does not create a new angular frequency from a single sinusoidal input. The output keeps the same coefficient of $t$; only the gain and phase can change.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the eigenfunction form: if the input is $x(t)=e^{j\\omega_0 t}$, then the output is $y(t)=H(j\\omega_0)e^{j\\omega_0 t}$. The factor $H(j\\omega_0)$ can change magnitude and phase, but it does not change $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y(t)=B\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi+\\theta)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So for a real sinusoid, read the input frequency from the coefficient of $t$ and keep that same $\\omega_0$ in the output. The amplitude may become $B$, and the phase may shift by $\\theta$, but the angular frequency stays fixed for the later examples in this lesson.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Same-Frequency Output Property",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Output Angular Frequency from a Single Sinusoidal Input",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system receives $x(t)=2\\cos(5t-\\pi/3)$. What angular frequency must appear in the output sinusoid?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient of $t$ inside the input sinusoid. For $x(t)=2\\cos(5t-\\pi/3)$, that coefficient is $5$, so the input angular frequency is $\\omega_0=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2\\cos(5t-\\pi/3) \\;\\Rightarrow\\; y(t)=B\\cos(5t+\\theta)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a continuous-time LTI system, the amplitude $2$ and the phase $-\\pi/3$ can change, but the angular frequency does not. So the output must still use $5$ as the coefficient of $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the output angular frequency is $\\omega_0=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Same-Frequency Output Property",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Same Frequency Despite Amplitude Changes",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system turns $x(t)=\\cos(7t)$ into $y(t)=3\\cos(7t-\\pi/4)$. Which part of the output changed, and which part stayed the same?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient of $t$ in the input cosine. Here $x(t)=\\cos(7t)$, so the input angular frequency is $\\omega_0=7$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\cos(7t) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y(t)=3\\cos(7t-\\pi/4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The factor $3$ changes only the amplitude, and the phase shift $-\\pi/4$ changes only the starting angle. The coefficient of $t$ is still $7$, so the output keeps the same angular frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the part that changed is amplitude, and the part that stayed the same is the angular frequency $7$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Same-Frequency Output Property",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Same Frequency Despite Phase Shifts",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system turns $x(t)=4\\sin(3t+\\pi/6)$ into $y(t)=4\\sin(3t-\\pi/3)$. What changed, and what stayed fixed?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient of $t$ in the input sine. Here $x(t)=4\\sin(3t+\\pi/6)$, so the input angular frequency is $\\omega_0=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=4\\sin(3t+\\pi/6) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y(t)=4\\sin(3t-\\pi/3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The phase changes from $\\pi/6$ to $-\\pi/3$, so the starting angle shifts. The coefficient of $t$ is still $3$, so the output keeps the same angular frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the phase changed, and the angular frequency stayed fixed at $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Same-Frequency Output Property",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Rejecting Output Sinusoids with New Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system receives $x(t)=\\cos(2t)$. Which proposed output cannot be correct because it changes the angular frequency?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient of $t$ in the input. Here $x(t)=\\cos(2t)$, so the input angular frequency is $\\omega_0=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\cos(2t) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y(t)=A\\cos(2t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Any proposed output with a different coefficient of $t$ is impossible. For example, a term like $\\cos(4t-\\pi/3)$ or $\\cos(t+\\pi/6)$ would change the angular frequency, so it cannot come from this input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the candidates that preserve $2t$, even if their amplitude or phase changes. A sign flip such as $-\\cos(2t)$ still has angular frequency $2$ because it only adds a phase shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
